    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The development of fractures in the sedimentary layers of Sheep Mountain anticline, a Laramide asymmetric fault-cored fold of the Bighorn Basin, is documented and interpreted as a method of constraining the structural evolution of the fold. This study suggests the existence of a regional fracture set predating the Laramide compression, and striking N110°E, oblique to the future fold. A set of joints, striking N45°E, present in the hinge and the backlimb, is associated with the NE oriented Laramide compression. Joints striking N135°E (fold parallel) are found within the hinge and are interpreted to have developed in response to the bending of stratigraphic layers. Two final fracture sets are attributed to a late stage of fold growth: a new joint set in the backlimb striking parallel to the set I fractures, but vertical in orientation; and a set of fractures in the forelimb representing the reactivation of set I fractures in reverse motion. The role of pre-existing fractures is thus illustrated in the context of both their influence on the initiation of new fractures and their reactivation. Furthermore, the present interpretation suggests that there was no lateral propagation of the fold and no hinge migration. It also illustrates that in some structural locations, limb rotation replaced the flexing of limbs, common in other structural locations.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
